     "text": "**Short answer:** It's a solid offer, but not an exceptional one, especially since Google recruited you while you were happy where you are. Don't accept it as-is. Negotiate, and push hardest on the parts that are guaranteed: new-hire equity and a sign-on bonus. Walk away only if Google won't improve the guaranteed numbers enough to justify leaving a job you like.\n\n## What the offer is really worth\n\nAll figures assume flat stock prices and bonuses paid at target.\n\n| | Meta (stay) | Google, no refreshes | Google, with the recruiter's illustrated refreshes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Year 1 | ~$526k (only ~$476k is already granted) | $552k | $552k |\n| Year 2 | ~$526k | $552k | $615k |\n| Year 3 | ~$526k | $552k | $677k |\n| Year 4 | ~$526k | $552k | $740k |\n| Year 5 | ~$526k | **$282k** | $532k |\n| 4-year gain vs. Meta | \u2014 | ~+$100k | ~+$480k |\n\nWhat this means:\n\n1. **In Year 1, the year that's most certain, you gain only about $26k (5%).** Changing jobs carries real risk: a new team, rebuilding your reputation, and performance ratings that drive your bonus and refreshes. That gain doesn't pay for much risk.\n2. **Most of the attractive numbers depend on refreshes, which aren't guaranteed.** The recruiter's $458k Year 4 equity figure depends on three good refresh cycles in a row. Your first refresh may also be prorated, depending on your start date.\n3. **Don't compare Google's rising line to Meta's flat $526k without care.** Your Meta $250k/yr already includes refreshes that have built up over time. Google's eventual steady state (about $532k) is basically the same as Meta's. The real advantage is a temporary bump in Years 2\u20134, while the new-hire grant overlaps with new refreshes.\n4. **The base salary is ordinary.** $235k is just above the midpoint of the $183k\u2013$271k range. That leaves room to go up.\n\n## Your leverage\n\nGoogle approached you, you're happy at Meta, and you're giving up unvested equity, including $200k in the next 12 months. You may also lose part of your Meta bonus, depending on when Meta pays it and when you'd leave. That's strong leverage, and you can be honest about it: \"I wasn't looking. To make a move, the guaranteed numbers need to be clearly better.\"\n\n## What to ask for\n\n| Item | Offer | Ask | Realistic outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Base | $235k | **$260k** | $245\u2013255k |\n| New-hire RSUs | $1.08M | **$1.35M** (+25%) | $1.2\u20131.3M |\n| Sign-on bonus | $0 | **$100k** (ideally split over Years 1\u20132) | $50\u201375k |\n\n- **At your ask:** Year 1 comes to about $750k.\n- **At a realistic outcome** ($250k base, $1.25M RSUs, $50k sign-on): Year 1 comes to about $660k. That's more than 25% above Meta, and it's guaranteed instead of depending on refreshes.\n\nEquity and the sign-on bonus usually have more room to move than base, because base is tied to pay bands. If Google holds firm on base, shift the request into equity.\n\n**A possible script:** \"I'm genuinely excited about the role. I wasn't looking, though, and leaving means giving up significant unvested Meta equity and bonus. The refresh illustration was helpful, but since it isn't guaranteed, I can't count it. If you can get to $260k base, $1.35M in equity and a $100k sign-on, I'm ready to sign.\"\n\n## Also confirm, in writing\n\n- The vesting schedule: whether there's a cliff, and whether it vests monthly or quarterly.\n- How the dollar grant is converted into shares (which stock-price average they use).\n- What happens to the sign-on bonus if you leave early (the clawback terms).\n- How refresh timing and proration work for your start date.\n- Your total unvested Meta equity beyond the next 12 months, so you know exactly what you'd give up.\n\n## How to decide\n\nSet your walk-away line ahead of time. For example: guaranteed comp for Years 1\u20132 should be at least 15\u201320% above staying at Meta. If Google won't move and you're left with a 5% gain in Year 1, plus a risky Year 5 if refreshes don't come through, staying where you're happy is reasonable. You could also ask Meta to counter, but only if you're truly prepared to leave, because asking can change how you're seen there.",
