    {
     "text": "**Yes. This offer clears the bar I suggested, and it beats the realistic outcome I predicted for Year 1.** If you're comfortable leaving a job you like, I'd accept after checking a few terms in writing. When a recruiter says \"best offer\" after already improving it this much, they usually mean it. Pushing again would likely gain little and could cost goodwill.\n\n## The new numbers\n\nThese figures assume flat stock, a bonus paid at target (now $48k) and the whole sign-on paid in Year 1.\n\n| | Meta (stay) | Google, no refreshes | Google, with illustrated refreshes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Year 1 | ~$476\u2013526k | **$758k** | $758k |\n| Years 2\u20134 (per year) | ~$526k | $558k | $620k \u2192 $683k \u2192 $745k |\n| Year 5 | ~$526k | $288k | $538k |\n| 4-year total | ~$2.05\u20132.1M | **~$2.43M** | ~$2.81M |\n\n(The Meta range in Year 1 is because only $200k of RSUs vest in the next 12 months, versus your ~$250k/yr average.)\n\n**Why it works:**\n- **Year 1 is up about $230\u2013280k (roughly 45\u201355%).** Your forfeited RSUs are fully replaced in cash, which is more certain than stock.\n- **Years 1\u20132 together come to about $1.32M versus about $1.0\u20131.05M at Meta.** That's 25\u201330% higher, well above the 15\u201320% threshold.\n- **Even with no refreshes at all, you're ahead about $330\u2013380k over 4 years.**\n\n**What still holds you back:**\n- **After Year 1, your guaranteed edge is thin.** It's only about $32k/yr in Years 2\u20134. The bigger upside depends on refreshes, which means you need to perform well at Google.\n- **Year 5 drops sharply if refreshes don't come through.** That's the same risk as before.\n- **The 4-year guaranteed total is a bit lower than a bigger RSU grant would have given** (about $70k less than the \"realistic outcome\" I sketched). In exchange, the money arrives up front and in cash, which is a fair trade.\n\n## Check these before signing\n\n1. **Sign-on payment and clawback.** Ask whether the $200k is paid all at once or split over Years 1\u20132. Find out what you'd have to repay if you leave early (typically a prorated amount within 1\u20132 years).\n2. **Tax withholding on the sign-on.** It's usually withheld at the flat 22% federal supplemental rate plus California's supplemental rate. That's probably less than you'll actually owe, so plan for a bill at tax time.\n3. **Your Meta vest and bonus dates.** Meta RSUs typically vest quarterly around the 15th of Feb, May, Aug and Nov. Check your schedule, because a vest may be only days away. Don't resign until after it lands. Also check whether you'd lose an upcoming Meta bonus payout by leaving before it's paid. If so, consider pushing your start date back rather than asking for more money.\n4. **RSU details.** Confirm the vesting cadence, whether there's a cliff, how the dollar amount converts to shares, and how your first refresh is prorated based on your start date.\n\n## Final decision\n\nFinancially, this is a clearly better offer, mainly because of a large, guaranteed Year 1. The remaining question isn't about money. It's whether the role, team and manager at Google are worth leaving a job you're happy in, given that most of the long-term upside depends on doing well there. If they are, take it. You could also ask Meta to counter, but only if you'd truly accept a Meta counter and are fine with the possible effect on how you're seen there.",
